// ©AngelaMos | 2026
// tsconfig.json.example
//
// Annotated TypeScript configuration for Cybersecurity-Projects frontends.
// Targets TypeScript 7 (the native Go compiler, shipped as `typescript`).
// Same 3-file layout `pnpm create vite --template react-ts` generates:
//
//   tsconfig.json       solution file: compiles nothing, references the two
//   tsconfig.app.json   browser code in src/ (DOM, React, vite/client)
//   tsconfig.node.json  build tooling (vite.config.ts) running on Node
//
// Why split: browser and Node code have different globals (window vs
// process) and different module semantics. One combined config lets
// `process.env` type-check inside a React component and then crash at
// runtime in the browser.
//
// `tsc -b` (build mode) walks the references, type-checks each project and
// caches results in node_modules/.tmp/*.tsbuildinfo.
//
// tsconfig files are JSONC: `//` comments are legal and are the ONLY safe
// way to annotate them. A "// key": "..." entry inside compilerOptions is an
// unknown compiler option, and tsc fails with TS5023.
//
// Copy all three into frontend/ and drop the .example suffix.
//
// Docs: https://www.typescriptlang.org/tsconfig/
// TS 7: https://devblogs.microsoft.com/typescript/announcing-typescript-7-0/
{
  // The solution file owns no source; it only points at the real projects.
  "files": [],
  "references": [
    { "path": "./tsconfig.app.json" },
    { "path": "./tsconfig.node.json" }
  ]
}
